# Results Structure

After KPI calculation you can `get_results` in format like below:
* fist key is a KPI NAME, eg: `kpi1a`, `kpi1b`
* Single kpi result has following main keys:
    * `start_points` - all start point events - it helpful for debugging - check what was parsed.
    * `end_points` - all end point events - it helpful for debugging - check what was parsed.
    * `start_point_timestamp` - it's usually first timestamp from `start_points` - but not always(depends on KPI definition)  
    * `end_point_timestamp` - it's usually last timestamp from `end_points` - but not always(depends on KPI definition)
    * `delta` = `end_point_timestamp - start_point_timestamp`
    * `operation_triggered_from` - MRBTS-<ID> of BTS where operation was triggered. Used for reporting KPI
    * `detailed_results` - detailed results split per RAT - sometimes it could be useful because we are interested particular RAT technology
    * `site_reset_timestamp` - timestamp or None if KPI is not related to site reset
    * `alarms` - contains all alarms which were `before`/`after` and during KPI calculation
    * `first_cell_down` - first cell down timestamp
    * `last_cell_down` - last cell down timestamp
    * `first_cell_up` - first cell up timestamp
    * `last_cell_up` - last cell up timestamp
    * Optional keys:
      * `cell_detailed_results` - similar like `detailed_results` but calculated for cells instead of RATs. It's available only for KPIs when start_point is related to changing of cell state
      * `indication_point` - indication point entry with `timestamp`, `dist_name` and `value` - It's available only when KPI has defined `start_search_after`
      * `device_info` - contains full information about reset device eg RU, BMOD
      * `hwf` - only for KPI1a/b/c - information if site reste was with HWF or not
      * `bts_detailed_results` - detailed results split per BTS - available only when `merge_ims_files` is used
* If you have more iteration, you can calculate average results by use `get_average_results` KW

Also if you want, you can save results to json file by `save_results_to_file` - it could be helpful for further analysis/debugging
<br>For better understanding structure please check sample file: [example_2.json](examples/generated_json_files/example_2.json)


# Adding new KPI
* Please contact tomasz.krol@nokia.com in case KPI you need is not defined yet.
* Please also prepare:
    * Sample IMS snapshot
    * KPI name/Requirement ID
    * Start/End points

# Example Usages
## Calculate single KPI
```python
from taf.ul.oam_kpi_calculations import oam_kpi_calculations
oam_kpi = oam_kpi_calculations()
oam_kpi.calculate_kpi1a(ims_file_path='/home/ute/IMS/KPI_site_reset.ims2')
oam_kpi.save_results_to_file(file_path='/home/ute/IMS/example_1.json')
```
Sample json file: [example_1.json](examples/generated_json_files/example_1.json)

## Calculate a few KPIs
```python
from taf.ul.oam_kpi_calculations import oam_kpi_calculations
oam_kpi = oam_kpi_calculations()
oam_kpi.calculate_kpis(kpi_names=['kpi1a', 'kpi1b', 'kpi1c'], ims_file_path='/home/ute/IMS/KPI_site_reset.ims2')
results = oam_kpi.get_results() # You can use it it for further actions like: report/verification 
oam_kpi.save_results_to_file(file_path='/home/ute/IMS/example_2.json')
```
Sample json file: [example_2.json](examples/generated_json_files/example_2.json)

## Calculate all iterations from one IMS snapshot
If you perform lets's say 20 iterations of lock/unlock block/unblock you can collect one IMS snapshot and calculate results at the end of the test.<br>

```python
from taf.ul.oam_kpi_calculations import oam_kpi_calculations
oam_kpi = oam_kpi_calculations()
oam_kpi.calculate_kpis(kpi_names=['kpi17a', 'kpi17b'], ims_file_path='/home/ute/IMS/block_unblock.ims2')
results = oam_kpi.get_results() # You can use it it for further actions like: report/verification 
avg_results = oam_kpi.get_average_results()
oam_kpi.save_results_to_file(file_path='/home/ute/IMS/example_3.json')
```
Sample json file: [example_3.json](examples/generated_json_files/example_3.json)

Of course some of iterations could be failed or not parsed correctly then such iteration also will be in results but `delta` for such iteration will be `None` like below:
```json
        {
            "delta": null, 
            "detailed_results": {}, 
            "end_point_timestamp": null, 
            "end_points": {
                "NR": {
                    "/MRBTS-1/RAT-1/RUNTIME_VIEW-1/MRBTS_R-1/NRBTS_R-78138/NRCELL_R-1": [
                        "2022-06-27 09:20:40.949000"
                    ]
                }
            }, 
            "indication_point": {}, 
            "site_reset_timestamp": null, 
            "start_point_timestamp": null, 
            "start_points": {
                "NR": {
                    "/MRBTS-1/RAT-1/URI-1/SESSION-53489/PLAN_VALIDATE_IND-1/DOWNLOAD_PLAN_TASK-246001/CHANGE_CONTEXT-0/NRCELL_A-1": [
                        "2022-06-27 09:20:20.954000"
                    ], 
                    "/MRBTS-1/RAT-1/URI-1/SESSION-53489/PLAN_VALIDATE_IND-1/DOWNLOAD_PLAN_TASK-246001/CHANGE_CONTEXT-0/NRCELL_A-2": [
                        "2022-06-27 09:20:20.954000"
                    ]
                }
            }
        }
```
Above iteration is failed because in the start point was locked 2 cell - but only one cell went UP.
Full results you can find [failed_iteration.json](examples/generated_json_files/failed_iteration.json)
In such situation also `OamKpiCalculationsException` will be raised

## Save KPIs into one file
```python
from taf.ul.oam_kpi_calculations import oam_kpi_calculations
oam_kpi = oam_kpi_calculations()
oam_kpi.calculate_kpi1a(ims_file_path='/home/ute/IMS/KPI_site_reset.ims2')
oam_kpi.calculate_kpi17a(ims_file_path='/home/ute/IMS/block_unblock.ims2', global_cell_id=54)
oam_kpi.save_results_to_file(file_path='/home/ute/IMS/example_4.json')
```
Sample json file: [example_4.json](examples/generated_json_files/example_4.json)

## Calculate KPI only from passed timestamp range
>__Note__
>
>Please use `save_search_start_timestamp` and `save_search_end_timestamp` KeyWords very carefully.
>When you have significant  time offset between BTS and your UTE VM then KPI may not be calculated.

```python
from taf.ul.oam_kpi_calculations import oam_kpi_calculations
oam_kpi = oam_kpi_calculations()
oam_kpi.save_search_start_timestamp()
# Do other staff
oam_kpi.save_search_end_timestamp()
# collect IMS snapshot
oam_kpi.calculate_kpi1a(ims_file_path='/home/ute/IMS/KPI_site_reset.ims2')
results = oam_kpi.get_results() # You can use it it for further actions like: report/verification 
oam_kpi.save_results_to_file(file_path='/home/ute/IMS/example_1.json')
```
Sample json file: [example_1.json](examples/generated_json_files/example_1.json)

## Calculate KPI from IMS from 2 BTSs
It is mostly used for RF sharing - then the best way is calculate KPI from both BTS
- to enbale this feature, you need to use `merge_ims_files=True` in `calculate_kpis` method

```python
from taf.ul.oam_kpi_calculations import oam_kpi_calculations

oam_kpi = oam_kpi_calculations()
ims_file_paths = ["/home/ute/ims/IMS_20240212_130134_4G.ims2",
                  "/home/ute/ims/IMS_20240212_130134_5G.ims2"]
oam_kpi.calculate_kpis(kpi_names=['kpi1a'],
                       ims_file_path=ims_file_paths,
                       merge_ims_files=True)
oam_kpi.save_results_to_file(file_path='/home/ute/temp/ims/kpi1a.json')
```

- then in results `bts_detailed_results` key is available - contains detailed results split per BTS.
  - you can choose which `delta` is important for you - eg main `delta` which including cells from both BTSs or delta only from particular BTS
```json
{
    "bts_detailed_results": {
        "MRBTS-1234": {
            "cell_detailed_results": {
                "/MRBTS-1/RAT-1/RUNTIME_VIEW-1/MRBTS_R-1/LNBTS_R-47212/LNCEL_R-1": {
                    "delta": "0:03:59.855000",
                    "end_point": "2024-02-12 13:06:19.903000",
                    "start_point": "2024-02-12 13:02:20.048000"
                },
                "/MRBTS-1/RAT-1/RUNTIME_VIEW-1/MRBTS_R-1/LNBTS_R-47212/LNCEL_R-2": {
